15:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1d",null,{"guide":{"date":"March 28–July 8, 1862","place":"Glorieta Pass, Santa Fe, Albuquerque, Peralta, and the Rio Grande valley in New Mexico Territory","result":"Confederate troops drove John Slough’s force from the field at Glorieta Pass, but John Chivington’s detachment destroyed their wagon train and ammunition at Johnson’s Ranch. The loss forced Henry Sibley to abandon Santa Fe and Albuquerque and retreat to Texas. The last Confederate force left New Mexico in July 1862","facts":[{"label":"Battle of Glorieta Pass","value":"March 28, 1862"},{"label":"Main forces","value":"About 850 U.S. troops under Slough; about 1,000 Confederates under Scurry"},{"label":"Flanking force","value":"About 490 under Chivington and Manuel Chaves"},{"label":"Confederate wagons destroyed","value":"About 80"},{"label":"Battle losses stated in episode","value":"U.S.: 47 killed, 78 wounded; Confederate: 42 killed, about 60 wounded"},{"label":"Albuquerque evacuated","value":"April 12, 1862"},{"label":"Confederate campaign loss stated in episode","value":"About 1,500 of roughly 3,500 Texans did not return"},{"label":"Strategic result","value":"U.S. victory; Confederate invasion of New Mexico ended"}],"maps":[{"title":"Glorieta Pass battlefield today","caption":"The March 28 fighting followed the Santa Fe Trail near Pigeon’s Ranch. Chivington’s detachment crossed the high ground south of the pass to reach the Confederate rear at Johnson’s Ranch.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-105.90%2C35.48%2C-105.55%2C35.72&layer=mapnik&marker=35.5727%2C-105.7500"},{"title":"Battle of Glorieta Pass","caption":"The National Park Service map shows Slough and Scurry fighting along the Santa Fe Trail while Chivington moved around the southern flank and destroyed the Confederate supply train at Johnson’s Ranch.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/the-battle-of-glorieta-pass-a-shattered-dream-teaching-with-historic-places.htm","image":"/images/apache-canyon-glorieta-map.jpg","alt":"National Park Service map of Glorieta Pass showing the main battle and Chivington’s flanking movement to Johnson’s Ranch"},{"title":"The campaign and retreat","caption":"This map identifies Glorieta, Santa Fe, Albuquerque, Peralta, Fort Craig, Mesilla, and Fort Bliss along the Confederate retreat route.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/resource/g3701sm.gcw0065000/?sp=64","image":"/images/sibley-new-mexico-campaign-map.jpg","alt":"Historical map of Sibley’s campaign in New Mexico"},{"title":"The Southwest in 1862","caption":"The National Park Service map shows the Confederate advance from Texas, the Colorado route to Fort Union, and the California Column’s eastward approach.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/the-battle-of-glorieta-pass-a-shattered-dream-teaching-with-historic-places.htm","image":"/images/new-mexico-campaign-nps-map.jpg","alt":"National Park Service map of the Civil War campaign routes across New Mexico and neighboring territories"}],"moments":[{"time":"1:23","audioTime":83,"title":"The main forces assembled","detail":"William Scurry had about 1,000 Confederates at Johnson’s Ranch. John Slough commanded about 1,300 U.S. troops east of Glorieta Pass."},{"time":"4:15","audioTime":255,"title":"Slough divided his force","detail":"On March 28, Slough took about 850 men west on the Santa Fe Trail while Chivington led roughly 490 men south of the pass with New Mexico guides."},{"time":"5:19","audioTime":319,"title":"Scurry advanced without his wagon train","detail":"Scurry left about 80 wagons under a small guard at Johnson’s Ranch and moved east into Glorieta Pass."},{"time":"6:27","audioTime":387,"title":"The main battle began","detail":"The forces met about half a mile west of Pigeon’s Ranch at about 11 a.m. Slough placed artillery across the trail and sent skirmishers onto both slopes."},{"time":"7:22","audioTime":442,"title":"The Confederates turned Slough’s first line","detail":"Scurry stopped a U.S. flanking move at an irrigation ditch while Pyron advanced around the opposite flank. Slough withdrew toward Pigeon’s Ranch."},{"time":"8:32","audioTime":512,"title":"U.S. artillery disabled Confederate guns","detail":"Fire from Artillery Hill knocked out two Confederate pieces and sharpshooters reduced the crews of the remaining gun."},{"time":"9:06","audioTime":546,"title":"Scurry ordered a general attack","detail":"The Confederate attacks against the U.S. center and Artillery Hill failed, and Major John Shropshire was killed."},{"time":"10:36","audioTime":636,"title":"The Confederate left forced another withdrawal","detail":"Texans on the northern slopes turned the U.S. line. Henry Raguet was mortally wounded, and Slough formed a third position east of Pigeon’s Ranch."},{"time":"11:14","audioTime":674,"title":"Slough left the battlefield","detail":"After about six hours of fighting, the exhausted U.S. force withdrew to Kozlowski’s Ranch. The Confederates held the field."},{"time":"11:50","audioTime":710,"title":"Chivington destroyed the Confederate supplies","detail":"Guided by Manuel Chaves, Chivington crossed 16 miles of mountain terrain, descended on Johnson’s Ranch, and burned about 80 wagons with food, ammunition, forage, tents, and medical stores."},{"time":"13:07","audioTime":787,"title":"Both armies reported heavy losses","detail":"The episode gives U.S. losses as 47 killed and 78 wounded and Confederate losses as 42 killed and about 60 wounded."},{"time":"14:09","audioTime":849,"title":"The supply loss decided the campaign","detail":"The Confederates returned to Santa Fe with only about 20 days of food and 35 to 40 cartridges per man."},{"time":"16:10","audioTime":970,"title":"Canby advanced from Fort Craig","detail":"Canby moved north with 1,200 men on April 1 and coordinated with the Fort Union force to retake Santa Fe and pressure Albuquerque."},{"time":"17:49","audioTime":1069,"title":"U.S. troops reoccupied Santa Fe","detail":"The Coloradans entered Santa Fe without opposition on April 11. The Confederates evacuated Albuquerque the next day."},{"time":"20:00","audioTime":1200,"title":"Canby fought Green at Peralta","detail":"On April 15, U.S. troops pushed the 5th Texas into Peralta. A dust storm covered the Confederate withdrawal across the Rio Grande."},{"time":"21:16","audioTime":1276,"title":"The Confederates bypassed Fort Craig","detail":"Sibley took a 100-mile detour west of the fort. The waterless eight-day march broke down unit organization and cost more men and equipment."},{"time":"23:08","audioTime":1388,"title":"The California Column approached","detail":"James Carleton’s 2,350-man force advanced east from Fort Yuma. The last Confederate rearguard left New Mexico on July 8."},{"time":"25:14","audioTime":1514,"title":"The survivors returned to Texas","detail":"Men reached San Antonio through the summer. The episode states that about 1,500 of the roughly 3,500 Texans who went west did not return."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Edward R. S. Canby","command":"Department of New Mexico","subordinates":"Advanced from Fort Craig and coordinated the recovery of Santa Fe and Albuquerque"},{"commander":"John P. Slough","command":"Main force at Glorieta Pass","subordinates":"About 850 Colorado volunteers, regulars, and New Mexico troops"},{"commander":"John M. Chivington","command":"Flanking detachment","subordinates":"About 490 men guided by Manuel Chaves; destroyed the Confederate train"},{"commander":"James H. Carleton","command":"California Column","subordinates":"2,350 California volunteers and regular artillery moving east from Fort Yuma"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"Henry H. Sibley","command":"Army of New Mexico","subordinates":"Ordered the retreat from Santa Fe, Albuquerque, and New Mexico"},{"commander":"William R. Scurry","command":"Main force at Glorieta Pass","subordinates":"About 1,000 dismounted Texas cavalry"},{"commander":"Charles L. Pyron","command":"Confederate right","subordinates":"Advanced around the U.S. left near Pigeon’s Ranch"},{"commander":"Thomas Green","command":"5th Texas Mounted Volunteers","subordinates":"Commanded the rear guard at Peralta"}]}],"people":[{"name":"William R. Scurry","role":"Commanded the Confederate main force at Glorieta Pass","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"John P. Slough","role":"Commanded the U.S. main force and later resigned his commission","side":"United States"},{"name":"John M. Chivington","role":"Led the detachment that destroyed the Confederate supply train","side":"United States"},{"name":"Manuel Chaves","role":"Guided Chivington’s detachment across the mountains to Johnson’s Ranch","side":"United States"},{"name":"Edward R. S. Canby","role":"Directed the U.S. counteroffensive from Fort Craig","side":"United States","image":"/images/edward-canby.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018666403/"},{"name":"Henry H. Sibley","role":"Commanded the failed Confederate invasion and retreat","side":"Confederacy"}],"losses":[{"name":"Major John Shropshire","side":"Confederacy","role":"Commander in the attack on Artillery Hill","outcome":"Killed during the failed Confederate attack"},{"name":"Major Henry W. Raguet","side":"Confederacy","role":"Commander on the Confederate left","outcome":"Mortally wounded while his troops turned the U.S. position"}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/pigeons-ranch-1880.jpg","alt":"Pigeon’s Ranch beside the Santa Fe Trail in 1880","caption":"Pigeon’s Ranch in 1880. The main battle centered on the ranch, the road, and the surrounding slopes.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/the-battle-of-glorieta-pass-a-shattered-dream-teaching-with-historic-places.htm"},{"image":"/images/apache-canyon-glorieta-map.jpg","alt":"National Park Service map of Glorieta Pass","caption":"The complete battle map shows the main action and the destruction of the wagon train.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/the-battle-of-glorieta-pass-a-shattered-dream-teaching-with-historic-places.htm"},{"image":"/images/edward-canby.jpg","alt":"Portrait of Edward R. S. Canby","caption":"Canby’s converging movements forced the Confederate retreat from New Mexico.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018666403/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Civil War Podcast episode 99","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.libsyn.com/99-sibleys-new-mexico-campaign-part-the-fourth"},{"label":"National Park Service: Glorieta Pass teaching guide","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/the-battle-of-glorieta-pass-a-shattered-dream-teaching-with-historic-places.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Glorieta Pass battle summary","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=nm002"},{"label":"National Park Service: Civil War on El Camino Real","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/the-civil-war-fought-along-el-camino-real-de-tierra-adentro-nht.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: War and westward expansion","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/the-war-and-westward-expansion.htm"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Sibley’s campaign map","url":"https://www.loc.gov/resource/g3701sm.gcw0065000/?sp=64"}]}}]}]
